# =============================================================================
# Configuration reference - EVERY environment variable referenced-automation-ui reads, directly or through
# createPlaywrightConfig / the shared utils code (referenced-automation-utils).
# This file is documentation: nothing loads it. Copy the lines you need into
#
#   .env.<name>          committed, NO secrets      (.env.qa, .env.stage, .env.dev)
#   .env.<name>.local    git-ignored, secrets only  (template: .env.local.example)
#
# and pick the file with the ENV variable (default qa). Load order, later wins:
#   .env -> .env.<ENV> -> .env.<ENV>.local -> the real environment (shell / CI variables).
# The secret file is named after the environment: ENV=qa reads .env.qa.local, NOT .env.local.
#
# Every variable below is documented the same way, on the lines above it:
#   what it is for / Read from / Values / Default | Required | Secret / Example
# "Read from" matters: a variable marked "real environment only" is read straight from
# process.env, so a line for it in a .env file has NO effect - set it in the shell or in CI:
#   bash / zsh : LOG_LEVEL=debug npx playwright test
#   PowerShell : $env:LOG_LEVEL = "debug"; npx playwright test      (undo: Remove-Item Env:LOG_LEVEL)
#   cmd.exe    : set LOG_LEVEL=debug && npx playwright test
#
# TIP - quoting values (dotenv rules):
#   * A value that contains # (starts a comment: PASSWORD=pa#ss is read as "pa"), $, quotes, or leading/trailing
#     spaces must be wrapped in double quotes:  UI_PASSWORD="pa#ss $word 'x'"
#   * Inside double quotes \n becomes a line break, so a Windows value with backslashes (DOMAIN\new-user,
#     C:\new\dir) is safer unquoted or in single quotes:  APP_USER='DOMAIN\new-user'
#   * A value that itself contains a double quote goes in single quotes:  NOTE='say "hi"'
#   * A key with an empty value (KEY=) is set to the empty string, which is not the same as not set.
#
# Secrets (Secret: YES) belong in .env.<name>.local or the CI secret store - never in a committed file:
#   cp .env.local.example .env.qa.local          (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.local.example .env.qa.local)
#
# Shared versus repo-specific: everything up to "Test data" is shared - the same keys, with the same meaning, in every
# repo of the family (documented in referenced-automation-utils: .env.example and the README "Configuration reference").
# "Credentials" and "Install and CI" are specific to this repo. utils also has REMOTE_* keys (the remote desktop
# host), S3270_PATH and TN3270_TIMEOUT_MS (mainframe): ui does not use them, so they are not listed here.
#
# Variable lines below are commented out; remove the leading "# " to use one.
# Tests that check this file: tests/envDocs.spec.ts (every variable the code reads is here, and nothing else).
# =============================================================================


# ---- Environment selection ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which environment to run against = which .env.<name> files are loaded and what is shown as "env" in the report.
# Read from: the real environment only (it chooses the file, so a line ENV=... inside a file is ignored)
# Values: any name that has a .env.<name> file here: qa | stage | dev | your own   (a name with no file just loads no file)
# Default: qa | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ENV=stage
# ENV=qa

# Fallback name for ENV, for machines where another tool already owns ENV. ENV wins when both are set.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: same as ENV
# Default: unset (then qa) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ENVIRONMENT=stage
# ENVIRONMENT=qa


# ---- Target URL --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the web application. createPlaywrightConfig makes it Playwright's baseURL (this repo's baseUrlKey), so page.goto('/login') works.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: an absolute URL with scheme, e.g. https://qa.example.com
# Default: unset | Required: no for the config itself; yes for any test that opens a relative URL (the converter prints "BASE_URL should be ..." after a conversion) | Secret: no
# Example: BASE_URL=https://qa.example.com
# BASE_URL=https://qa.example.com


# ---- Browser and run selection (createPlaywrightConfig) ----------------------------------------------------------

# Which browser(s) to run. An unknown value fails at start-up with the list of valid names. In GitLab CI the test matrix sets it per leg (chromium, firefox, webkit).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: chromium | firefox | webkit | msedge | msedge-beta | msedge-dev | chrome | chrome-beta | chrome-dev | all (the three engines) | all-devices (the mobile devices)
# Default: chromium | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: BROWSER=firefox
# BROWSER=chromium

# Emulate one Playwright device instead of a desktop browser; wins over BROWSER. An unknown name fails at start-up.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: any name in Playwright's device list, e.g. "iPhone 15", "Pixel 7", "iPad Pro 11" (quote names with spaces)
# Default: unset (then BROWSER decides) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: DEVICE="iPhone 15"
# DEVICE=

# Run the browser without a window.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off; anything else, such as "ture", is an error)
# Default: true | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: HEADLESS=false
# HEADLESS=true

# How many test files run at the same time.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 1, or a percentage of the logical cores such as 50%
# Default: unset (Playwright uses half of the logical cores) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: WORKERS=4
# WORKERS=

# How often a failed test is run again before it counts as failed (a test that passes on a retry is reported as flaky).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 0
# Default: 0 on a developer machine; 1 when the CI variable is set | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: RETRIES=2
# RETRIES=0

# Stop the whole run after this many failed tests.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a whole number >= 0 (0 = never stop early)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: MAX_FAILURES=5
# MAX_FAILURES=0

# Slow every browser action down so a person can watch (debugging only).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number >= 0 (0 = off)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: SLOW_MO_MS=250
# SLOW_MO_MS=0

# Width of the browser window for desktop projects (device projects use their own preset size).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: pixels, a whole number
# Default: 1280 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: VIEWPORT_WIDTH=1920
# VIEWPORT_WIDTH=1280

# Height of the browser window for desktop projects.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: pixels, a whole number
# Default: 800 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: VIEWPORT_HEIGHT=1080
# VIEWPORT_HEIGHT=800

# Browser language (navigator.language, Accept-Language, date and number formats).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: a BCP 47 tag such as en-GB, de-DE, fr-CA
# Default: unset (the browser's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOCALE=en-GB
# LOCALE=

# Browser time zone, so date assertions do not depend on where the test machine is.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: an IANA zone name such as Europe/London, America/New_York, UTC
# Default: unset (the machine's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TIMEZONE_ID=Europe/London
# TIMEZONE_ID=

# Emulated prefers-color-scheme.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: light | dark | no-preference
# Default: unset (the browser's own) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: COLOR_SCHEME=dark
# COLOR_SCHEME=

# Accept invalid or self-signed HTTPS certificates (test environments only).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: true | false (also 1/0, yes/no, on/off)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: IGNORE_HTTPS_ERRORS=true
# IGNORE_HTTPS_ERRORS=false

# The attribute getByTestId() looks at.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: an HTML attribute name, e.g. data-testid, data-test, data-qa
# Default: data-testid | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_ID_ATTRIBUTE=data-test
# TEST_ID_ATTRIBUTE=data-testid


# ---- Timeouts, all in milliseconds (createPlaywrightConfig) ------------------------------------------------------

# How long one whole test may take.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 30000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How long a web-first assertion (expect(...)) waits for its condition.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 10000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
# EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# How long one action (click, fill, ...) waits for its element.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 15000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# How long a navigation (page.goto, link clicks) waits.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number
# Default: 30000 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: NAVIGATION_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# NAVIGATION_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How long the whole run may take before Playwright stops it.
# Read from: .env files or the real environment
# Values: milliseconds, a whole number (0 = no limit)
# Default: 0 | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: GLOBAL_TIMEOUT_MS=3600000
# GLOBAL_TIMEOUT_MS=0


# ---- Logging (logger/logger.ts in utils) -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Minimum level written to the console and the log file.
# Read from: the real environment only (the logger reads process.env once, when it is first imported; .env files are not read)
# Values: error | warn | info | http | verbose | debug | silly
# Default: info | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_LEVEL=debug
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Also write the JSON-lines log file (a CI artifact). If the file cannot be created the logger carries on with the console only.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: true | false (only the text "false", any case, turns it off)
# Default: true | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_TO_FILE=false
# LOG_TO_FILE=true

# Where the log file is written.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: a path, relative to the folder tests start in or absolute
# Default: logs/automation.log | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_FILE=logs/run-42.log
# LOG_FILE=logs/automation.log

# One log file per Playwright worker (logs/automation-w2.log) instead of every worker appending to one file.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: true | false (only the text "true", any case, turns it on)
# Default: false | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER=true
# LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER=false

# Switch coloured console output off. Colour is already off when CI is set or the output is not a terminal.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value turns colour off (the NO_COLOR convention)
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: NO_COLOR=1
# NO_COLOR=

# Switch coloured console output on even in CI or when the output is piped.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value turns colour on (even 0 or false - it is the presence that counts)
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: FORCE_COLOR=1
# FORCE_COLOR=


# ---- Test data -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Seeds the random-data generators (randomUtils, {{random:...}} placeholders in Gherkin steps) for every Playwright worker, so a
# failing run can be replayed with the same data. The seed in use is logged at start-up.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: an integer
# Default: unset (a different random seed every run) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: RANDOM_SEED=12345
# RANDOM_SEED=


# ---- Credentials (repo-specific) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How a test gets a password or token without writing it into a file. Every name below is just an environment
# variable that a test reads through loadEnv() (.env files or the real environment - both work).
#
#   * Gherkin: a value written {{env:NAME}} reads NAME, any name you choose (features/*.feature use {{env:UI_PASSWORD}}); the step
#     'I read the environment variable "NAME" into "var"' does the same. A value from {{env:...}} is masked in step titles and logs.
#     Names the CI system itself injects (job tokens, registry passwords) are refused by {{env:...}}.
#   * The recording converter (npm run codegen-to-tests): a value it recognises as a secret becomes env.get('UI_...').
#     Rule: UI_ + the field's accessible name (or its locator text) in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE, so the field "Password" is
#     UI_PASSWORD, "API token" is UI_API_TOKEN, "Enter PIN" is UI_ENTER_PIN. Other places a recording can hold a
#     credential get UI_ + the place: a URL parameter ?token=... is UI_URL_TOKEN, a cookie "session" is UI_COOKIE_SESSION,
#     a header is UI_AUTHORIZATION or UI_X_API_KEY, a storage key is UI_TOKEN. The same literal gets the same name, a
#     different literal under the same name gets a suffix (UI_PASSWORD_2). The command lists the names it used; set
#     exactly those. --param 'regex=NAME' picks your own name for a field.
# Only the fixed names are listed as variables below; the derived ones follow the rule above.

# The password the login in features/*.feature and in the generated tests under tests/generated/ types. The same name is what the converter
# derives for a field labelled "Password". This repo's own suite sets a made-up value for its local fixture page when it is unset (tests/support/recordedTest.ts).
# Read from: .env files or the real environment, through loadEnv() in the test
# Values: any text (wrap a value containing # $ or quotes in double quotes)
# Default: unset | Required: yes for a login scenario or generated test against a real application | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret, never a committed file
# Example (in .env.qa.local): UI_PASSWORD="replace-me"
# UI_PASSWORD=

# A "Bearer ..." value found in a recording (an Authorization header or code in an evaluated string); the converter writes env.get('UI_BEARER_TOKEN').
# Read from: .env files or the real environment, through loadEnv() in the generated test
# Values: the token text without the word Bearer
# Default: unset | Required: only if a converted recording used it | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret
# Example (in .env.qa.local): UI_BEARER_TOKEN="replace-me"
# UI_BEARER_TOKEN=

# A "Basic ..." value found in a recording; the converter writes env.get('UI_BASIC_CREDENTIAL').
# Read from: .env files or the real environment, through loadEnv() in the generated test
# Values: the base64 text without the word Basic
# Default: unset | Required: only if a converted recording used it | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret
# Example (in .env.qa.local): UI_BASIC_CREDENTIAL="replace-me"
# UI_BASIC_CREDENTIAL=

# A JWT (eyJ...) found in a recording; the converter writes env.get('UI_JWT').
# Read from: .env files or the real environment, through loadEnv() in the generated test
# Values: the whole token
# Default: unset | Required: only if a converted recording used it | Secret: YES - .env.<name>.local or a CI secret
# Example (in .env.qa.local): UI_JWT="replace-me"
# UI_JWT=


# ---- Set by the machine, not by you ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CI systems set this (GitLab: CI=true). It raises the retries default, forbids test.only, keeps all test output and turns console colour off.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: any non-empty value means "running in CI"
# Default: unset | Required: no (do not set it by hand unless you want the CI behaviour locally) | Secret: no
# Example: CI=1
# CI=

# Playwright sets this for every worker (0, 1, 2, ...); the logger prints it as [w2] and uses it for LOG_FILE_PER_WORKER. Do not set it yourself.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: a whole number, set by Playwright
# Default: unset outside a Playwright worker | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TEST_WORKER_INDEX=2
# TEST_WORKER_INDEX=

# The time zone of the Node process (dates, logs). Node itself reads it, and only from the real environment - a line in a .env file is NOT applied.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: an IANA zone name such as UTC or Europe/London
# Default: the machine's own time zone | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: TZ=UTC
# TZ=UTC


# ---- Install and CI (repo-specific; read by scripts/*.sh|.bat and .gitlab-ci.yml, before any .env file could be read) ----

# The npm registry that scripts/setup.sh|.bat and create-package install from; publish-package publishes to it. They run `npm config set registry` with it,
# and so does every GitLab CI job (a CI/CD variable).
# Read from: the real environment only (the scripts run before any .env file could be read)
# Values: the registry URL of your organisation, e.g. an Artifactory npm repository
# Default: unset (npm keeps the registry it already uses; publish-package refuses to run without it) | Required: no for setup, yes for publish-package and in CI | Secret: no
# Example: NPM_REGISTRY_URL=https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/api/npm/npm-virtual/
# NPM_REGISTRY_URL=

# Install the @automation/* packages from locally built tarballs (../shared-packages) instead of the registry. The same as setup --local.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: 1 turns it on; anything else leaves it off
# Default: unset (install from the registry) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: USE_LOCAL_PACKAGES=1
# USE_LOCAL_PACKAGES=

# Do not download Playwright's browsers: setup.sh|.bat skip their `npx playwright install` step, and Playwright's own installer honours it too.
# For a locked-down machine: set BROWSER to a browser that is already installed (msedge or chrome). create-package --local sets it itself.
# Read from: the real environment only
# Values: 1 skips the download
# Default: unset (browsers are downloaded) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=1
# PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=

# Mirror that Playwright downloads its browsers from, when the public download site is blocked (GitLab CI: a CI/CD variable; see the header of .gitlab-ci.yml).
# Read from: the real environment only (read by Playwright's installer)
# Values: the base URL of the mirror, e.g. an Artifactory generic remote repository
# Default: unset (Playwright's own download site) | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST=https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/playwright-cdn
# PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST=

# GitLab CI only: set it on a manual pipeline to make the `audit` job also run lint, typecheck, test and security (slow).
# Read from: the real environment only (a GitLab CI/CD variable of the pipeline)
# Values: any non-empty value turns it on
# Default: unset | Required: no | Secret: no
# Example: AUDIT_RUN_GATES=1
# AUDIT_RUN_GATES=
